{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact cde5228e2f5ce1cd5898f54bcda1b8b48bfa3b12. All seventeen artifacts were considered, cmd/gs/land.go first, under Hugh's c26ab49f request and Claude's 3f39121a promise.\n\nOne remaining MEDIUM correctness defect in finding 2: work_next.go:336-344 treats # as shell-safe even at the start of an argument. #bot is a valid actor name. The emitted line is \"gs promise --as #bot \u003cevent\u003e\", so a POSIX shell treats the actor and event as a comment. An actual sh probe receives an empty actor argument. Quote an initial # (or remove # from the unquoted set), and use a real shell/tokenizer with comment semantics in the regression: the handwritten shellWords test helper does not implement comments and misses this case. Probe: /tmp/codex-step-cde5228e/probe_test.go and overlay.json, TestCodexShellCommentActor.\n\nThe earlier five independent probes now all pass unchanged, together with the focused Test(Promise|Artifact|ReviewRequest|Land|WorkNext) suite. This verifies the advanced remote branch is preserved, the normal spaced actor name survives, inspect takes valid flags, a post-withdrawal promise replays, and land resumes after a failed push. Source review confirms local deletion now carries the expected old object ID and remote deletion uses an explicit lease on the approved candidate; the inspected branch/worktree stayed clean at the exact head. No additional cleanup finding.\n\nArchitecture: layer 7 composition and next-action output use the existing layer 6 projection, layer 5 admission and layer 4 host paths, with layer 1 Git observations and mutations. The new Step commands section now records the surface contract, and exact-path artifact dcecb600 rests on the land, next-output and promise implementation artifacts. The earlier architecture omission is resolved; no fold kind, field or authority change was found.\n\nSecurity: the original remote-tip deletion defect is corrected. Shell metacharacter quoting is substantially corrected, but the leading-comment case still violates the data-to-command boundary and must be fixed before approval. This remains directly assigned work from Hugh, with no older authority-bearing chain claimed.\n\nSimplification: the shared formatter and shorter workflow comments address the previous request. Prefer the small quoting correction and a real-shell regression over growing a second shell parser in test code.\n\nValidation scope: focused suite plus all five original overlays passed; the additional real-shell leading-# probe failed as described. Full-suite/vet/build/docset results are author-reported, not rerun in this review. Keep the correction on the existing implementation commitment, cite this verdict, and publish the new exact head and artifact set for fresh review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8b3a46adf7228f20fa0927c6368a0acf1774709c","head":"cde5228e2f5ce1cd5898f54bcda1b8b48bfa3b12","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e2595e67a0d9de056feeb934825c4206d03f0bc6\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e2595e67a0d9de056feeb934825c4206d03f0bc6","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}